          %p
            I am a
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://www.j-paine.org/#consultancy"} freelance programmer
            %a{:href => "http://www.j-paine.org/"} My Website
            says more about this and other things I do.
          %p
            But I was tempted into category theory, not least by my friends Hendrik Hilberdink,
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "http://www.imar.ro/~diacon/"} Răzvan Diaconescu
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://www.di.uminho.pt/~jbb/"} José Bernardo Barros
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Joseph+Goguen"} Joseph Goguen
          %p
            Using experience gained from writing
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "http://www.j-paine.org/#economics_and_distance_learning"} Web-based simulations of the economy
            = succeed ";" do
              %a{:href => "http://www.j-paine.org/cgi-bin/webcats/webcats.php"} here
            %a{:href => "http://www.j-paine.org/cgi-bin/webcats/set_prod_nlab.php"} one more
            that I’ve adapted to use the same notation as the nLab
            %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/product"} product
            page. If I could get funding, I’d like to make these easier for novices to learn from.
            %a{:href => "http://www.comlab.ox.ac.uk/people/Jamie.Vicary/"} Jamie Vicary
            offered to host them on the Oxford Computing Lab’s servers: a good next step would be to tidy up and document the whole system, then make the source publicly available.
          %p
            I’d also like to popularise category theory through my
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://www.j-paine.org/dobbs/index.html"} blog at Dr Dobbs
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2006/11/a_categorical_manifesto.html#c018171"} in an n-Category Café posting
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2006/11/a_categorical_manifesto.html#c018173", :title => "Section on neural networks from the above posting"} neural networks and their relation to logic and model theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2006/11/a_categorical_manifesto.html#c018180", :title => "Section on analogical reasoning from the above posting"} analogical reasoning
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2006/11/a_categorical_manifesto.html#c018555", :title => "Comment on holographic reduced representations from the above posting"} holographic reduced representations
          %p
            One benefit is the tools category theory gives us to “achieve independence from the often overwhelmingly complex details of how things are represented or implemented”. (I quote
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Joseph+Goguen"} Joseph Goguen
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a{:href => "http://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/viewdoc/summary?doi=10.1.1.13.362"}
                %cite A categorical manifesto
          %p Perhaps — I don’t know whether I’m joking or not — categorical characterisations could help us understand other phenomena. Could we find a universal property that a computational system satisfies if and only if it is conscious?!
          %p Or — a simpler question, and one that must be sensible — that it satisfies if and only if it can represent and reason about itself? The latter would be an interesting project in dynamical systems; but perhaps it’s assuming too much to regard the reasoner as a dynamical system.
          %p
            Category theory also gives us tools for unifying disparate mathematical and computational phenomena. AI and cognitive science, of course, are full of disparate mathematical and computational phenomena, usually ones whose relatedness is badly understood. That is why my n-Category Café posting above mentioned neural networks: in it, I cited Michael Healy’s paper
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/viewdoc/summary?doi=10.1.1.32.2635"}
                %cite Category Theory Applied to Neural Modeling and Graphical Representations
          %p
            As another example, I cited a paper by Goguen on analogical reasoning via “conceptual blending”:
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/viewdoc/summary;jsessionid=E7800C5B8537F1A3E2AD33585E735831?doi=10.1.1.75.2277"}
                %cite Mathematical Models of Cognitive Space and Time
          %p
            And as yet another, I suggest at
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalisation+as+an+adjunction"} generalisation as an adjunction
            that generalisation can be represented as an adjunction. More precisely, that generalisation and instantiation can be represented as an adjoint pair. Perhaps this could unify lots of different topics in machine learning.
          %p
            Read the following quote from
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a{:href => "http://gregegan.customer.netspace.net.au/"} Greg Egan
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://gregegan.customer.netspace.net.au/INCANDESCENCE/Incandescence.html"}
                %cite Incandescence
          %blockquote
            'Interesting Truths' referred to a kind of theorem which captured subtle unifying insights between broad classes of mathematical structures. In between strict isomorphism — where the same structure recurred exactly in different guises — and the loosest of poetic analogies, Interesting Truths gathered together a panoply of apparently disparate systems by showing them all to be reflections of each other, albeit in a suitably warped mirror. Knowing, for example, that multiplying two positive integers was really the same as adding their logarithms revealed an exact correspondence between two algebraic systems that was useful, but not very deep. Seeing how a more sophisticated version of the same could be established for a vast array of more complex systems — from rotations in space to the symmetries of subatomic particles — unified great tracts of physics and mathematics, without collapsing them all into mere copies of a single example.
          %p
            Incidentally, category theory also inspired me to invent a way of
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "http://www.j-paine.org/#safe_spreadsheets_fast_with_excelsior"} building spreadsheets from modules
            %a{:href => "http://www.spreadsheet-parts.org/"} storing these modules on the Web
            for anyone to download into their own spreadsheets.
          .query
            %p
              Note the Markdown method for block-level code (which cannot produce a blank line at the beginning or end of the block), now also at the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sandbox"} Sandbox
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Toby+Bartels"} Toby Bartels
